Given this simple test:

```python
def test():
    x = 1
    assert x == 3
```

This is the produced AST (courtesy of `pytest-ast-back-to-python`):

```
import builtins as @py_builtins
import _pytest.assertion.rewrite as @pytest_ar

def test():
    x = 1
    @py_assert2 = 3
    @py_assert1 = x == @py_assert2
    if @py_assert1 is None:
        from _pytest.warning_types import PytestAssertRewriteWarning
        from warnings import warn_explicit
        warn_explicit(PytestAssertRewriteWarning('asserting the value None, please use "assert is None"'), category=None, filename='c:\\pytest\\.tmp\\test-foo.py', lineno=3)
    @py_format4 = @pytest_ar._call_reprcompare(('==',), (@py_assert1,), ('%(py0)s == %(py3)s',), (x, @py_assert2)) % {'py0': @pytest_ar._saferepr(x) if 'x' in @py_builtins.locals() or @pytest_ar._should_repr_global_name(x) else 'x', 'py3': @pytest_ar._saferepr(@py_assert2)}
    @py_format6 = '%(py5)s' % {'py5': @py_format4}
    if not @py_assert1:
        raise AssertionError(@pytest_ar._format_explanation('' + ('assert ' + @py_format6)))
    elif @pytest_ar._check_if_assertionpass_impl():
        @pytest_ar._call_assertion_pass(3, 'x == 3', @pytest_ar._format_explanation(@py_format6))
    @py_format4 = @py_format6 = None
    @py_assert1 = @py_assert2 = None
```

Above we are generating the `@py_format4` and `@py_format6` variables even if we do not end up using them, in the case the assertion is true and there are no hook implementations. 

I remeber it was written like this prior to the `_check_if_assertionpass_impl` check, which made sense: they would always be used, so why not generating them once? Given that it is no longer the case, it seems better we only generate the intermediate variables if we will use them, something like:

```
    if not @py_assert1:
        @py_format4 = @pytest_ar._call_reprcompare(('==',), (@py_assert1,), ('%(py0)s == %(py3)s',), (x, @py_assert2)) % {'py0': @pytest_ar._saferepr(x) if 'x' in @py_builtins.locals() or @pytest_ar._should_repr_global_name(x) else 'x', 'py3': @pytest_ar._saferepr(@py_assert2)}
        @py_format6 = ('' + 'assert %(py5)s') % {'py5': @py_format4}
        raise AssertionError(@pytest_ar._format_explanation(@py_format6))
    elif @pytest_ar._check_if_assertionpass_impl():
        @py_format4 = @pytest_ar._call_reprcompare(('==',), (@py_assert1,), ('%(py0)s == %(py3)s',), (x, @py_assert2)) % {'py0': @pytest_ar._saferepr(x) if 'x' in @py_builtins.locals() or @pytest_ar._should_repr_global_name(x) else 'x', 'py3': @pytest_ar._saferepr(@py_assert2)}
        @py_format6 = '%(py5)s' % {'py5': @py_format4}
        @pytest_ar._call_assertion_pass(3, 'x == 3', @pytest_ar._format_explanation(@py_format6))
    @py_format4 = @py_format6 = None
    @py_assert1 = @py_assert2 = None
```

This way we don't incurr the cost of generating the variables for every test, even if they won't be used. Of course there's duplicated code, but it's all generated anyway so it shouldn't be a problem.

(Sorry for dragging this review for so long. This is a sensitive part of the code so I'm trying to be as comprehensive as possible, as any mistake here might potentially affect a large portion of our user base, I'm sure you understand.)

